  "construction": "Leave-one-geopolitical-zone-out cross-validation of the LGA model. Each fold drops every household in one of the six zones, refits the identical household-level binomial model (ten design columns of eight covariates, BYM2 on the queen-contiguity graph over all 774 LGAs built once from the full boundary file, IID cluster effect, INLA 25.10.19 defaults), then predicts the held-out zone's LGAs from prediction rows placed at each LGA's mean covariate profile taken from the FULL household file (covariates held known) with cluster index NA. theta_s is the LGA prevalence with the cluster effect marginalised by the same 7-point Gauss-Hermite rule as scripts/lga_model.R. 95% intervals come from the marginal posterior of the linear predictor (mean and sd from summary.linear.predictor, Gaussian on the logit scale) mapped through that integral on a 2001-point standard-normal probability grid; no Monte Carlo draws are used, so the intervals carry no simulation noise. The truth is the direct unweighted proportion from the held-out households of each LGA; its variance is a cluster-robust ratio linearisation with strata = state and PSU = cluster (hv001). LGAs with a single cluster have no estimable design variance and are imputed the fold's median design effect on the binomial variance. z = (model_mean - direct) / sqrt(model_var + design_var). Pass rule fixed before any fold was fitted: mean(z) in [-0.15, 0.15], sd(z) in [0.85, 1.15], share |z| <= 1.96 in [0.90, 0.98], pooled over the 654 LGAs that hold at least one cluster. The 120 unclustered LGAs have no direct estimate and are excluded. State and zone coverage aggregate LGA posteriors with household weights and treat the LGAs as independent, which understates the aggregate interval width; that secondary count is therefore biased against the model, not for it. Count-functional calibration is NOT tested: it needs joint draws per fold.",
